@*
    A page rendered straight from a form POST (the cart after +/-, login or register with an
    error, a checkout step, an admin save that failed validation...) sits in history as that
    POST. Every such page carries an antiforgery token, so it is sent no-store and is never
    kept: Back or refresh has to send the POST again, and Chrome stops on its own "Confirm Form
    Resubmission" (ERR_CACHE_MISS) screen - inside the installed app too, with no way out.

    This turns the entry into a plain GET of the same address the moment the page starts to
    load, so Back and refresh simply load the page again. Done once here rather than as a
    redirect in every action: thirty-odd store actions, admin, and plugins whose code we do not
    own all render views from a POST, and a redirect would also lose their validation messages.
    Every one of those addresses also answers a GET (nopCommerce pairs them by ActionName).
*@
@if (Microsoft.AspNetCore.Http.HttpMethods.IsPost(Context.Request.Method))
{
    <script>
        history.replaceState(history.state, '', location.href);
    </script>
}